`fi`'s long formats append the partitive ending as a literal: `LL` is `Do MMMM[ta] YYYY`, so formatting writes `15. lokakuuta 2024` while the month token still only knows the nominative `lokakuu`. Under strict parsing the month token consumes the whole word and the `[ta]` literal no longer matches, so every long date this locale formats fails to parse back:

```js
moment([2024, 9, 15]).format('LL');
// '15. lokakuuta 2024'
moment('15. lokakuuta 2024', 'LL', 'fi', true).isValid();
// false
moment('tiistai, 15. lokakuuta 2024, klo 14.25', 'LLLL', 'fi', true).isValid();
// false
```

The month regex overrides stop the month token before the partitive ending, leaving `ta` for the literal, the same way other locales narrow their month tokens. The lenient regexes mirror `computeMonthsParse`, so both parsing modes tokenize the long names consistently.

Tests: a new `parse long date` test formats `LL` for all twelve months and checks that each value parses back with the original month and day, plus an `LLLL` round trip. On develop it fails 25 assertions; it passes with the change. Focused run: 25 tests, 1183 assertions. Full suite: 3945 tests, 165066 assertions, all green. `eslint`, `prettier --check`, and `scripts/check-months-parse.js` are clean.

