Two strings this locale writes could not be parsed back.

### Meridiem

`meridiem()` emits `a.t.m.`/`p.t.m.`, but `meridiemParse` is `/[ap]\.t\.m/i`, which stops at the `m`. In strict mode the unconsumed trailing dot invalidates the match:

```js
moment(new Date(2010, 1, 14, 15, 25, 50)).locale('eo').format('YYYY-MM-DD h:mm:ss a');
// '2010-02-14 3:25:50 p.t.m.'
moment('2010-02-14 3:25:50 p.t.m.', 'YYYY-MM-DD h:mm:ss a', 'eo', true).isValid();
// false
```

Making the internal dots optional and allowing the trailing one restores the round trip. It also accepts `atm`/`ptm`, the abbreviated day periods CLDR gives for Esperanto ([common/main/eo.xml](https://github.com/unicode-org/cldr/blob/7afa5cdb6b7fe7ae65f934c6dbaa374f25b5a616/common/main/eo.xml#L1688-L1692)), which the old regex rejected as well.

### Weekday followed by a literal

`LLLL` is `dddd[n], ...`, so this locale writes `mardon`, `dimanĉon`, and so on. Without `weekdaysParseExact`, the strict weekday token takes the whole word `mardon`; the exact day-name comparison then fails, so every `LLLL` value fails to parse:

```js
moment([2024, 9, 15, 14, 25]).format('LLLL');
// 'mardon, la 15-an de oktobro, 2024 14:25'
moment('mardon, la 15-an de oktobro, 2024 14:25', 'LLLL', 'eo', true).isValid();
// false
```

`weekdaysParseExact: true` makes the weekday token match one of the listed names, leaving the `n` for the literal.

### Tests

- New `parse meridiem` block covering both dotted cases, both `a`/`A` cases, the undotted CLDR form, and a format→strict-parse round trip. Five assertions fail on current `develop` and pass after the change.
- New `parse weekday followed by a literal` block covering all seven `dddd[n]` forms plus an `LLLL` round trip. Eight assertions fail without the flag and pass with it.
- The existing `format` expectations are untouched. Full suite green (3946 tests, 165055 assertions), `eslint` and `prettier --check` clean.

